=======================================================
SQL NOTES: DML COMMANDS (Data Manipulation Language)
=======================================================

DML commands work on the DATA inside tables -- not the structure.
You use DML to add, change, and remove rows.

DML Command  | What it does
--------------------------------------
INSERT       | Add new rows to a table
UPDATE       | Modify existing rows
DELETE       | Remove specific rows

NOTE: Unlike DDL, DML changes CAN be undone using ROLLBACK
      if you are inside a transaction. More on this at the end.

=======================================================
1. INSERT -- add new rows into a table
=======================================================

-------------------------------------------------------
Way 1: Insert WITHOUT specifying column names
-------------------------------------------------------

    INSERT INTO users VALUES
    (1, 'Alice',  'alice@gmail.com',  'Male', '2000-05-09'),
    (2, 'Alice2', 'alice2@gmail.com', 'Male', '2001-08-14');

    You must provide values for EVERY column in the table,
    in the EXACT order the columns were defined during CREATE TABLE.

    Problem with this approach:
        - If you add a new column to the table later, this query breaks.
        - You are forced to provide values even for AUTO_INCREMENT columns,
          which defeats the purpose of AUTO_INCREMENT.
        - Easy to mix up column order and insert wrong data silently.

    When to use: quick one-off inserts during testing only.

-------------------------------------------------------
Way 2: Insert WITH specifying column names (RECOMMENDED)
-------------------------------------------------------

    INSERT INTO users (name, email, gender, dob) VALUES
    ('Alice',  'alice@gmail.com',  'Male', '2000-05-09'),
    ('Alice2', 'alice2@gmail.com', 'Male', '2001-08-14'),
    ('Alice3', 'alice3@gmail.com', 'Male', '2002-03-21');

    You list only the columns you want to provide values for.
    Columns you skip get their value automatically:
        - AUTO_INCREMENT column (id) -- MySQL assigns the next number
        - DEFAULT column (created_at) -- MySQL uses the default value
        - Nullable column -- MySQL stores NULL if no default is set

    Why this is the best way:
        - You don't need to provide id -- MySQL handles it.
        - Adding new columns to the table later won't break this query.
        - Column order in your INSERT does not have to match table order.
        - Clear and readable -- you can see exactly what is being inserted.

-------------------------------------------------------
INSERT common mistakes to avoid
-------------------------------------------------------

    -- WRONG: values count does not match columns listed
    INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Alice');
    -- ERROR: Column count doesn't match value count

    -- WRONG: inserting a gender value not in ENUM list
    INSERT INTO users (name, email, gender) VALUES ('Alice', 'a@b.com', 'attack helicopter');
    -- ERROR: Data truncated for column 'gender'

    -- WRONG: inserting duplicate email (UNIQUE constraint)
    INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Bob', 'alice@gmail.com');
    -- ERROR: Duplicate entry for key 'email'

    -- WRONG: skipping a NOT NULL column with no DEFAULT
    INSERT INTO users (email) VALUES ('alice@gmail.com');
    -- ERROR: Field 'name' doesn't have a default value

=======================================================
2. UPDATE -- modify existing rows
=======================================================

-------------------------------------------------------
Basic syntax
-------------------------------------------------------

    UPDATE table_name
    SET    column1 = value1, column2 = value2, ...
    WHERE  condition;

-------------------------------------------------------
WARNING: UPDATE without WHERE
-------------------------------------------------------

    UPDATE users SET salary = 45000;

    This updates EVERY row in the table.
    Every single user's salary becomes 45000.
    There is no automatic undo.

    When is this actually useful:
        When you genuinely want to set a column to the same value
        across ALL rows. For example:
            -- Give everyone a default DOB if none was set:
            UPDATE users SET dob = '1999-01-01' WHERE dob IS NULL;

    Rule of thumb: always write your WHERE clause FIRST before running UPDATE.

-------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE a single row
-------------------------------------------------------

    UPDATE users SET salary = 45000 WHERE id = 1;

    Only the row where id = 1 is affected.
    Using PRIMARY KEY in WHERE is the safest way to target one exact row.

-------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE multiple columns at once
-------------------------------------------------------

    UPDATE users SET salary = 45000, email = 'newemail@gmail.com' WHERE id = 1;

    Separate multiple column assignments with a comma inside SET.
    All changes apply to the same row(s) matched by WHERE.

-------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE using the existing value (calculated update)
-------------------------------------------------------

    UPDATE users SET salary = salary + 10000 WHERE salary < 60000;

    salary + 10000 reads the current salary and adds 10000 to it.
    This is called a calculated update -- you don't need to know the current value.
    Only rows where salary is below 60000 are affected.

-------------------------------------------------------
More UPDATE examples
-------------------------------------------------------

    -- 1. Update name using email as the identifier:
    UPDATE users SET name = 'Rob the Bob' WHERE email = 'xyz@gmail.com';

    -- 2. Give a raise to all underpaid employees:
    UPDATE users SET salary = salary + 10000 WHERE salary < 60000;

    -- 3. Update multiple rows matching a condition:
    UPDATE users SET is_active = FALSE WHERE dob < '1950-01-01';

    -- 4. Update using BETWEEN:
    UPDATE users SET salary = 50000 WHERE id BETWEEN 5 AND 10;

=======================================================
3. DELETE -- remove specific rows from a table
=======================================================

-------------------------------------------------------
Basic syntax
-------------------------------------------------------

    DELETE FROM table_name WHERE condition;

-------------------------------------------------------
Delete a specific row
-------------------------------------------------------

    DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 1;
    DELETE FROM users WHERE email = 'alice@gmail.com';

    Only the row(s) matching the WHERE condition are deleted.
    Using PRIMARY KEY in WHERE is safest -- targets exactly one row.

-------------------------------------------------------
Delete rows matching a condition
-------------------------------------------------------

    DELETE FROM users WHERE salary < 65000;
    DELETE FROM users WHERE is_active = FALSE;
    DELETE FROM users WHERE dob < '1950-01-01';

    All rows satisfying the condition are deleted.

-------------------------------------------------------
WARNING: DELETE without WHERE
-------------------------------------------------------

    DELETE FROM users;

    Deletes every single row in the table.
    The table structure (columns, constraints) stays intact.
    AUTO_INCREMENT counter is NOT reset (it continues from where it stopped).

-------------------------------------------------------
DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP (important comparison)
-------------------------------------------------------

    Command             | Removes data | Removes structure | WHERE allowed | Resets AUTO_INCREMENT
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    DELETE FROM users   | Yes          | No                | Yes           | No
    TRUNCATE TABLE users| Yes          | No                | No            | Yes
    DROP TABLE users    | Yes          | Yes               | No            | N/A (table gone)

    DELETE   -- surgical, row by row, can filter with WHERE, can be rolled back
    TRUNCATE -- wipes all rows at once (faster), resets counter, harder to roll back
    DROP     -- destroys the entire table including its structure

-------------------------------------------------------
How to drop the entire table
-------------------------------------------------------

    DROP TABLE users;

    This does NOT just delete the data.
    It deletes the TABLE ITSELF -- structure, columns, constraints, everything.
    After this, even SELECT * FROM users will throw an error (table doesn't exist).

    Safe version:
    DROP TABLE IF EXISTS users;
    -- No error if the table doesn't already exist.

=======================================================
TRANSACTIONS (bonus -- ties DML together)
=======================================================

DML commands (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) can be wrapped in a transaction.
A transaction lets you group multiple DML operations and either:
    COMMIT   -- save all changes permanently
    ROLLBACK -- undo all changes as if they never happened

    START TRANSACTION;

    UPDATE users SET salary = 99999 WHERE id = 1;
    DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 2;

    -- something went wrong? undo everything:
    ROLLBACK;

    -- everything looks good? save permanently:
    COMMIT;

    Why this matters:
        Without a transaction, every DML command auto-commits immediately.
        With a transaction, you have a safety net before making it permanent.
        Critical for banking, order processing, any multi-step operation.

=======================================================
DML QUICK REFERENCE
=======================================================

Task                              | Query
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Insert one row (named columns)    | INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Alice', 'a@b.com');
Insert multiple rows              | INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (...), (...), (...);
Update one row by ID              | UPDATE users SET salary = 50000 WHERE id = 1;
Update multiple columns           | UPDATE users SET salary = 50000, email = 'x@y.com' WHERE id = 1;
Calculated update                 | UPDATE users SET salary = salary + 5000 WHERE salary < 40000;
Delete one row                    | DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 1;
Delete rows by condition          | DELETE FROM users WHERE salary < 30000;
Delete all rows (keep structure)  | DELETE FROM users;
Wipe all rows + reset counter     | TRUNCATE TABLE users;
Delete table entirely             | DROP TABLE users;

